Locally authored vz diagnostic correction for pinned youki 0.7.0.
Apply AFTER runtime-audit.patch. Not an upstream-reviewed backport.

The notify socket is bound by a RELATIVE name after a process-global chdir, to
stay inside the 108-byte sun_path limit. When that bind fails, the error keeps
the errno in a `source` field the Display impl never prints, so the whole
message is the socket's random name:

    failed to bind notify socket: tenant-notify-f7a93802.sock

That is the entire diagnostic for a failure that has cost this project several
composed release candidates (`vz-mzs.7.4`), and it does not say which errno, nor
whether the chdir that the relative name depends on was actually in effect. This
prints the errno, the directory the code intended to be in, and the directory it
was actually in. It changes no behaviour.

--- a/crates/libcontainer/src/notify_socket.rs
+++ b/crates/libcontainer/src/notify_socket.rs
@@ -15,10 +15,15 @@
     #[error("invalid path: {0}")]
     InvalidPath(PathBuf),
-    #[error("failed to bind notify socket: {name}")]
+    // vz: the relative bind depends on a process-global chdir having taken
+    // effect, so report the errno and both the intended and observed cwd.
+    #[error("failed to bind notify socket {name}: {source} (intended cwd {workdir}, observed cwd {observed})")]
     Bind {
         source: std::io::Error,
         name: String,
+        workdir: PathBuf,
+        observed: PathBuf,
     },
     #[error("failed to connect to notify socket: {name}")]
     Connect {
@@ -67,8 +72,12 @@
         let stream = UnixListener::bind(socket_name).map_err(|e| NotifyListenerError::Bind {
             source: e,
             // ok to unwrap here as OsStr should always be utf-8 compatible
             name: socket_name.to_str().unwrap().to_owned(),
+            workdir: workdir.to_owned(),
+            observed: env::current_dir().unwrap_or_else(|_| PathBuf::from("<unreadable>")),
         })?;
